511
00:36:13,150 --> 00:36:25,230
So what CORS is, is
cross-origin resource sharing.

512
00:36:25,230 --> 00:36:28,830
It's basically
making sure that you

513
00:36:28,830 --> 00:36:36,110
can't pull files or resources
on the same machine,

514
00:36:36,110 --> 00:36:37,530
from the same machine.

515
00:36:37,530 --> 00:36:39,150
And what that does
is it makes it--

516
00:36:39,150 --> 00:36:46,050
it's a way to protect your
computer, your EC2 instance,

517
00:36:46,050 --> 00:36:49,990
or any other machine that
you have a server running.

518
00:36:49,990 --> 00:36:55,590
It helps protect it from
certain types of cyber attacks.

519
00:36:55,590 --> 00:36:59,590
And so what we're going to
need to do to set up Django

520
00:36:59,590 --> 00:37:05,270
to allow CORS-- since we're
running this as a development

521
00:37:05,270 --> 00:37:07,510
server, you wouldn't do this.

522
00:37:07,510 --> 00:37:11,830
In prod, your-- like,
if you were running

523
00:37:11,830 --> 00:37:17,190
an app in production, you would
have your Django server running

524
00:37:17,190 --> 00:37:21,970
on one machine,
at one URL or IP,

525
00:37:21,970 --> 00:37:30,070
and then your app frontend
would be hosted somewhere else,

526
00:37:30,070 --> 00:37:32,930
so you wouldn't
have CORS errors.

527
00:37:32,930 --> 00:37:35,430
But while we do
have this set up,

