00:49:28,320 --> 00:49:30,520
gets hit first, OK.

690
00:49:30,520 --> 00:49:33,920
So this one says, hey, I've
got this thing called api.

691
00:49:33,920 --> 00:49:39,520
And this API endpoint also
has these added on to it,

692
00:49:39,520 --> 00:49:41,920
api_app.urls.

693
00:49:41,920 --> 00:49:43,840
So we can now open that.

694
00:49:43,840 --> 00:49:47,090
And then we've got this users
endpoint and a users endpoint

695
00:49:47,090 --> 00:49:50,430
with an integer at the end.

696
00:49:50,430 --> 00:49:55,450
Well, api/users is what we've
got, so we've got a match.

697
00:49:55,450 --> 00:49:59,750
Since we've got a match, we're
going to open up this view.

698
00:49:59,750 --> 00:50:02,510
This view will
handle the request.

699
00:50:02,510 --> 00:50:05,730
So let's go ahead and open up
this UserListCreateAPIView.

700
00:50:08,290 --> 00:50:13,250
Over here-- look at that,
UserListCreateAPIView.

701
00:50:13,250 --> 00:50:17,810
And it's made with our
generics from REST framework.

702
00:50:17,810 --> 00:50:20,860
That's basically all
the code that we need.

703
00:50:20,860 --> 00:50:25,970
And REST framework writes
the rest of it for us.

704
00:50:25,970 --> 00:50:28,190
And then we have our Serializer.

705
00:50:28,190 --> 00:50:34,370
We need the serializer because,
remember, in our slice,

706
00:50:34,370 --> 00:50:37,890
we had to stringify this, right?

707
00:50:37,890 --> 00:50:40,450
So it's a string, it's not JSON.

708
00:50:40,450 --> 00:50:48,130
Python-JSON is not the
same as JavaScript-JSON.

709
00:50:48,130 --> 00:50:52,130
So in our view, we've got a
serializer to help handle that.

710
00:50:52,130 --> 00:50:54,690
And Django REST
framework kind of

711
00:50:54,690 --> 00:50:56,990
handles the rest of
the logic for us.

712
00:50:56,990 --> 00:51:02,650
But at the end of the
day, it uses our models.py

713
00:51:02,650 --> 00:51:07,910
to say, hey, have you got
a table for this user?

714
00:51:07,910 --> 00:51:08,410
Yes.

715
00:51:08,410 --> 00:51:11,730
And then we match it up.

716
00:51:11,730 --> 00:51:16,900
The data that we've got gets
matched to these columns.

717
00:51:16,900 --> 00:51:18,650
First_name, last_name,
phone_number, these

718
00:51:18,650 --> 00:51:21,130
are all columns.

719
00:51:21,130 --> 00:51:24,770
They're character fields,
in other words, strings that

720
00:51:24,770 --> 00:51:28,150
match a length of 100 or less.

721
00:51:28,150 --> 00:51:34,710
So if I had submitted some name
with more than 100 characters,

722
00:51:34,710 --> 00:51:36,770
we would see this fail.

723
00:51:36,770 --> 00:51:40,270
We would get a bad request
response from Django.

724
00:51:43,370 --> 00:51:49,070
And then after that, it
gets added to the database,

725
00:51:49,070 --> 00:51:52,490
suppose it gets added to
the database successfully.

726
00:51:52,490 --> 00:51:55,710
Our view, Django REST framework
again handles this for us.

727
00:51:55,710 --> 00:52:00,650
But it just responds,
saying, hey, I got that data,

728
00:52:00,650 --> 00:52:03,450
you get a 200
status, and I'm going

729
00:52:03,450 --> 00:52:08,610
to give you the entire object
you gave me back to you

730
00:52:08,610 --> 00:52:11,810
in response.

731
00:52:11,810 --> 00:52:23,550
So back in our slice, let's
go down to the fulfilled.

732
00:52:23,550 --> 00:52:26,450
Oh, actually, let's handle--

733
00:52:26,450 --> 00:52:28,910
in our createUser
POST, so at this point,

734
00:52:28,910 --> 00:52:31,890
yeah, Django responded to us.

735
00:52:31,890 --> 00:52:35,050
The asynchronous waiting
period here is over.

736
00:52:35,050 --> 00:52:36,810
We've come to the next line now.

737
00:52:36,810 --> 00:52:41,790
If not response.ok-- well,
we did get an OK response,

738
00:52:41,790 --> 00:52:44,810
that data was sent successfully,
and added to the database,

739
00:52:44,810 --> 00:52:48,770
and it gave it back to us.

740
00:52:48,770 --> 00:52:50,470
So we're not throwing
an error here.

741
00:52:50,470 --> 00:52:52,830
But if we got an error, this
is where it would happen.

742
00:52:52,830 --> 00:52:56,110
And then we return
response.json.

743
00:52:56,110 --> 00:53:02,330
So we return whatever
data it gave back to us.

744
00:53:02,330 --> 00:53:07,810
And then that triggers
the fulfilled event, which

745
00:53:07,810 --> 00:53:09,910
is caught by our extraReducers.

746
00:53:09,910 --> 00:53:16,730
So if we go down here to the
createUser.fulfilled event,

747
00:53:16,730 --> 00:53:18,210
then we have an action.

748
00:53:18,210 --> 00:53:23,270
That action.payload is that JSON
that the server gave back to us.

749
00:53:23,270 --> 00:53:31,770
So we add to our existing list
of form users that user that got

750
00:53:31,770 --> 00:53:33,330
returned to us.

751
00:53:33,330 --> 00:53:38,530
So we had a form here.

752
00:53:38,530 --> 00:53:40,150
We pushed the data to Django.

753
00:53:40,150 --> 00:53:42,110
Django responded
with the same data.

754
00:53:42,110 --> 00:53:46,590
So Django got the copy that
got added to the database.

755
00:53:46,590 --> 00:53:47,430
We got a copy.

756
00:53:47,430 --> 00:53:49,410
We added it to our Redux store.

757
00:53:49,410 --> 00:53:52,790
So now, we both have a
copy of the same data.

758
00:53:55,570 --> 00:53:58,610
So that's how it
goes full circle.

759
